  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Pecan」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pecan#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1369467020,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "Pecans are one of the most recently domesticated of the major crops. Although wild pecans were well known among native and colonial Americans as a delicacy, the commercial growth of pecans in the United States did not begin until the 1880s. As of 2014, the United States produced an annual crop of 119.8 million kilograms (264.2 million pounds), with 75% of the total crop produced in Georgia, New Mexico, and Texas. They can be grown from USDA hardiness zones approximately 5 to 9, and grow best where summers are long, hot and humid. The nut harvest for growers is typically around mid-October. \nThe tree requires little pruning because it naturally forms a vase-shaped canopy; the only pruning necessary is to remove diseased, broken, or dead limbs.\nIn 2017, outside the U.S., Mexico produced nearly half of the world's total, similar in volume to that of the U.S., together accounting for 93% of global production. As of 2024, South Africa is the third largest producer, mostly exporting to China. Pecan trees require large quantities of water during the growing\nseason, and most orchards in the region use flood irrigation to optimize consumptive water use and production of mature pecans. Generally, two or more trees of different cultivars must be present to pollinate each other.\n\n Choosing cultivars can be a complex practice, based on the Alternate Bearing Index (ABI) and their period of pollinating. Commercial growers are most concerned with the ABI, which describes a cultivar's likelihood to bear on alternating years (index of 1.0 signifies the highest likelihood of bearing little to nothing every other year).",
        "truncated": true
      }
    }
  },
